# Tropes Reference — [Project Name]

## Genre(s): [e.g., Solarpunk Thriller / Cozy Mystery / Secondary World Fantasy]

Populated at project setup by running research-assistant against the project's genre pages on TV Tropes.
Update the "In active use" section as chapters are written.

---

## Genre-native tropes
Tropes readers expect in this genre. Use some — they're genre contracts and reader pleasure.
The goal is intentional deployment, not avoidance. A familiar trope executed well is satisfying;
the same trope deployed by default, without thought, is a cliché.

- **[Trope Name]** (tvtropes.org/…) — [what it is; what reader expectation it creates]
- **[Trope Name]** — [description]
- ...

---

## Clichés to avoid
Overused executions in this genre that readers have seen too many times without payoff.
Subvert, invert, or skip. If used, the execution must earn it.

- **[Trope Name]** — [why it's tired; what makes it feel like a default rather than a choice]
- **[Trope Name]** — [description of the failure mode]
- ...

---

## In active use (this manuscript)
Tropes deliberately deployed as the story is written. Updated after each chapter or arc.

| Trope | Chapter | How deployed | Subverted? |
|-------|---------|-------------|------------|
| [Name] | Ch. 1 | [straight / variant] | [no / yes — how] |

---

## Subversions and inversions
Tropes we're deliberately playing against reader expectation. Tracking these prevents
unintentional double-subversion (subverting the same trope twice in a row stops being subversion).

- **[Trope Name]** (Ch. X): Reader expects [X]; we do [Y] instead.

---

## Setup notes
To populate this file at project start:
1. Run research-assistant with the project genre(s) and these TV Tropes pages as targets:
   - The genre's main page (e.g., tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/SolarpunkGenre)
   - Any subgenre pages relevant to this project
   - The "Played Straight / Subverted / Averted" sections of 3–5 central genre tropes
2. Distill into the sections above — roughly 8–15 genre-native tropes, 5–10 clichés to avoid
3. Add any project-specific tropes the synopsis or plan already commits to
